<p class="gna-lede">Botulinum toxin injections — often called by the brand name Botox — into the chewing muscles can reduce the force of jaw clenching and grinding for some people, and may ease related muscle pain and headaches. But the research is mixed, the effect wears off after a few months, and the treatment does not protect your teeth the way a splint does. For most people, a custom night splint and self-care are the sensible first step, with injections considered in selected cases.</p>

<h2 id="short-answer">Does Botox help clenching and grinding?</h2>
<p>Clenching or grinding the teeth is called <strong>bruxism</strong>. It comes in two broad forms: <em>awake bruxism</em>, usually clenching during the day when concentrating or under stress, and <em>sleep bruxism</em>, grinding or clenching during sleep that you may not know about until a family member hears it or a dentist notices worn teeth.</p>
<p>Bruxism on its own is common and does not always need treatment. It becomes a problem when it causes aching jaw muscles, morning headaches, worn or cracked teeth and fillings, sensitive teeth, or tiredness in the jaw when eating. In some people the masseter muscles at the angle of the jaw become noticeably enlarged.</p>
<p>Botulinum toxin can help some of these problems by weakening the muscles that do the clenching. It is one option among several — and usually not the first.</p>
<div class="gna-keypoints">
  <h3>Key points</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Botulinum toxin temporarily weakens the chewing muscles, reducing the force of clenching rather than stopping it.</li>
    <li>Some studies show reduced pain and grinding; others find little difference from a placebo. The evidence is genuinely mixed.</li>
    <li>Using it for bruxism is generally an off-label use, so informed consent and an experienced doctor matter.</li>
    <li>Effects typically last three to four months and need repeating.</li>
    <li>A custom splint protects the teeth, is reversible and remains the usual first step.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<h2 id="how-it-works">How masseter injections work</h2>
<p>Botulinum toxin type A is a purified protein that blocks the chemical signal between a nerve and the muscle it controls. Injected in small, measured doses into a muscle, it causes temporary, partial weakness in that muscle only. It does not affect sensation, so you can still feel your face normally.</p>
<p>For bruxism, the injections are usually placed in the <strong>masseter</strong> muscles, the thick muscles you can feel at the angle of the jaw when you clench. Some doctors also treat the <strong>temporalis</strong> muscles at the temples. The aim is to reduce the force the muscles can generate — enough to lessen strain and pain, but not so much that eating becomes difficult.</p>
<p>It is important to understand what this does not do. Bruxism is driven by the brain and nervous system, and is linked to stress, sleep patterns, caffeine, alcohol, smoking and some medicines. Botulinum toxin does not change those triggers. Studies suggest it mainly reduces the <em>intensity</em> of clenching, with less consistent effects on how <em>often</em> grinding episodes happen.</p>

<h2 id="evidence">What the evidence says</h2>
<p>This is an area where honest answers matter, because confident claims are common online.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A 2019 systematic review of four randomised trials concluded that injections reduced bruxism episodes, pain and biting force compared with placebo or traditional treatment.</li>
  <li>A 2024 systematic review and meta-analysis looking at jaw-muscle and jaw-joint disorders found that, overall, botulinum toxin was <strong>not significantly better than placebo</strong> for pain at one, three or six months, nor for mouth opening or bruxism events.</li>
  <li>Most trials are small, use different doses and injection sites, follow people for only a few months, and measure outcomes in different ways.</li>
</ul>
<p>Putting this together: some people with painful, persistent clenching do report meaningful relief, and the treatment can be reasonable to try in carefully selected adults. But it is not a dependable solution for everyone, and more high-quality research is needed. If someone promises you that it will definitely work, treat that as a warning sign.</p>

