<h2 id="off-label">Off-label use: what it means for you</h2>
<p>In most countries, bruxism is not among the officially licensed uses of botulinum toxin products, so using it for clenching or grinding is described as <strong>off-label</strong>. Off-label prescribing is common and legitimate in medicine when a doctor believes the potential benefit outweighs the risk, but it does mean:</p>
<ul>
  <li>There is no single agreed dose or injection pattern; it relies on the doctor's training and judgement.</li>
  <li>You should receive a clear explanation of the uncertain evidence, the alternatives and the risks before you consent.</li>
  <li>The product should be genuine, obtained through a licensed supplier and stored correctly, and the injections given by a qualified doctor in a clinical setting.</li>
  <li>Health insurance policies often do not cover out-patient injections of this kind — check your policy rather than assuming.</li>
</ul>
<p>Cosmetic slimming of an enlarged masseter (sometimes called jawline contouring) is a related but separate reason for the same injections. If appearance is part of your motivation, say so, so that the plan can be discussed openly. You can read more about our <a href="/botox-treatment">Botox treatment</a> service.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-expect">What to expect from treatment</h2>
<h3>Assessment first</h3>
<p>Before any injection, your jaw muscles, jaw joints, teeth and bite should be examined. Pain in this area can come from the jaw joint itself, from the teeth, from the sinuses or from nerves, and botulinum toxin will not help these. Your doctor will also ask about medical conditions, pregnancy or breastfeeding plans, and medicines you take.</p>
<h3>The injections</h3>
<p>The procedure takes a few minutes in the clinic. A fine needle is used to place a few small injections into each masseter muscle, sometimes into the temples as well. No anaesthetic is usually needed; most people describe a brief sting. You can usually return to normal activities straight away, but avoid massaging the area, lying flat or strenuous exercise for the rest of the day, as your doctor advises.</p>
<h3>After the injections</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Days 2–7:</strong> the effect begins. You may notice chewing tough food is more tiring.</li>
  <li><strong>Around 2 weeks:</strong> the effect is close to its peak. A review at this stage is common.</li>
  <li><strong>Around 3–4 months:</strong> the effect gradually fades as nerve connections recover. Some people find it lasts longer.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you decide to repeat it, the dose and spacing should be reviewed each time. Very frequent or high-dose injections may increase the chance of the body forming antibodies that make the treatment less effective.</p>

<h2 id="risks">Side effects and risks</h2>
<p>Most side effects are mild and temporary, but you should know about them before deciding:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Common:</strong> soreness, bruising or swelling at the injection sites; headache; tiredness or weakness when chewing hard or chewy foods.</li>
  <li><strong>Less common:</strong> an uneven or altered smile if the toxin affects nearby facial muscles; a bulge when clenching from uneven muscle weakening; dry mouth.</li>
  <li><strong>Changes in face shape:</strong> a slimmer lower face as the masseter reduces in bulk, which may be unwanted. With repeated treatment, some people notice a hollower appearance at the angle of the jaw.</li>
  <li><strong>Long-term questions:</strong> some studies suggest that repeated injections may reduce bone density in the jaw, because the bone is loaded less. The clinical significance is not yet clear.</li>
  <li><strong>Rare:</strong> allergic reactions, and very rarely effects spreading beyond the injection area.</li>
</ul>
<div class="gna-callout gna-callout--warn">
  <p><strong>Seek urgent medical help (call 108 or go to the nearest emergency department)</strong> if, after injections, you develop difficulty swallowing, speaking or breathing, drooping eyelids, double vision or widespread muscle weakness.</p>
</div>
<p>Botulinum toxin is generally not suitable if you are pregnant or breastfeeding, have a neuromuscular condition such as myasthenia gravis, have an infection at the injection site, or have had a previous reaction to the product. Certain medicines, including some antibiotics, can interact with it.</p>

<h2 id="splint-vs-botox">When a splint makes more sense</h2>
<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Custom night splint</th><th>Botulinum toxin injections</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>What it does</td><td>Spreads biting forces and creates a protective barrier between the teeth</td><td>Temporarily weakens the clenching muscles</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Protects teeth from wear</td><td>Yes</td><td>Indirectly, by reducing force; not a physical barrier</td></tr>
    <tr><td>How long it lasts</td><td>Years, with adjustments and occasional replacement</td><td>About 3–4 months per treatment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reversible</td><td>Yes — remove it at any time</td><td>Wears off gradually; cannot be switched off early</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Main downsides</td><td>Needs to be worn consistently; some find it uncomfortable at first</td><td>Mixed evidence, repeat injections, chewing weakness, possible change in face shape</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Often suited to</td><td>Most people with grinding, tooth wear or jaw-joint symptoms</td><td>Selected adults with persistent, painful clenching or enlarged masseters</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>A splint is usually the more appropriate first step if your teeth are worn or cracking, if the main problem is night-time grinding, if your jaw joint clicks, catches or locks, or if you are pregnant or prefer to avoid medicines. Our guide to <a href="/blogs/tmj-splint-vs-surgery/">how TMJ splints work and when more is needed</a> explains this in detail.</p>
<p>Self-care matters whichever route you take: noticing and relaxing daytime clenching ("lips together, teeth apart"), reducing caffeine and alcohol late in the day, stopping smoking and tobacco, and addressing stress. Our article on <a href="/blogs/face-muscles-tightening-due-to-anxiety/">facial muscle tightness and anxiety</a> covers practical strategies.</p>
<p>Grinding at night can also be linked to disturbed sleep. If you snore loudly, wake up choking or feel very sleepy during the day, ask about a sleep assessment — see our guide on <a href="/blogs/snoring-vs-sleep-apnea-difference/">snoring vs sleep apnoea</a>.</p>

<h2 id="when-to-see-specialist">When to see a specialist</h2>
<p>See a dentist or maxillofacial surgeon if you have jaw-muscle pain that lasts more than a few weeks, morning headaches, teeth that are wearing down or cracking, a jaw that clicks painfully, catches or locks, or reduced mouth opening. These deserve a proper examination before any treatment is chosen, and the same symptoms can sometimes have causes other than bruxism.</p>
<p>At our clinic in Gachibowli, Hyderabad, we assess the muscles, joints, teeth and bite together and discuss the full range of options, from self-care and splints to injections where appropriate. Results vary between individuals, and the right plan is one you understand and agree with. For a wider look at jaw-muscle and joint problems, see our <a href="/tmj-treatment">TMJ treatment</a> page.</p>
